<h2>SMS and email strategies for auto repair</h2>

<p>The question is not whether to use SMS or email. It is knowing when to use each one. They serve different purposes, and shops that use both well will always outperform those that rely on just one.</p>

<p><strong>SMS is for urgency and convenience.</strong> Appointment reminders, service-due notifications, and time-sensitive offers. Open rates on text messages sit above 90 percent. When you text a customer that their car is ready for pickup, or that you have a cancellation slot available tomorrow, they see it within minutes. If you want to understand the full potential here, our breakdown of <a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">SMS marketing for small business</a> goes deep on timing, frequency, and what to avoid.</p>

<p><strong>Email is for value and education.</strong> Seasonal maintenance tips, explanations of common warning signs, monthly newsletters with car care advice. Email lets you be the trusted expert, not just the place that changes oil. It builds the relationship between visits. Our guide to <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email marketing for small business</a> covers how to write emails people actually open.</p>

<p>A few rules that apply to both channels:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Keep it short.</strong> Nobody wants a 500-word text message about brake fluid. Get to the point. One clear message per communication.</li>
  <li><strong>Personalise when possible.</strong> "Hi Sarah, your 2019 Mazda 3 is due for a tyre rotation" works ten times better than "Dear valued customer, it is time for service."</li>
  <li><strong>Always include a clear next step.</strong> A phone number to call. A link to book online. A reply-to-confirm option. Make it effortless to say yes.</li>
  <li><strong>Respect the frequency.</strong> One or two messages a month is plenty. More than that and you become noise. Less than that and you are forgotten.</li>
</ul>

<p>One practical tip: use SMS for reminders and confirmations, and use email for everything educational. That way your texts always feel useful, and your emails always feel valuable. Neither channel becomes spam.</p>

