<h2>Review generation: asking at the right moment</h2>

<p>Google reviews are the new word of mouth. When someone searches "mechanic near me," the shops with more five-star reviews show up first and get clicked first. This is not optional anymore. It is the most visible form of auto repair marketing that exists.</p>

<p>The challenge is that happy customers rarely leave reviews on their own. They mean to. They just forget. The trick is to ask at exactly the right moment, when the positive experience is freshest.</p>

<p>The best time to ask is right after service completion, ideally within two hours of pickup. A simple automated text works perfectly: "Thanks for choosing us today, Sarah. If you were happy with the service, a quick Google review would mean the world to us." Include a direct link to your Google review page. Make it one tap.</p>

<p>A few things to get right:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Only ask happy customers.</strong> If there was a complaint or a delay, fix the problem first. Sending a review request after a bad experience is asking for a one-star rating.</li>
  <li><strong>Make it effortless.</strong> The link should go directly to the review form, not your Google Business listing. Every extra click you add is a customer who gives up.</li>
  <li><strong>Do not offer incentives.</strong> Google's guidelines prohibit offering discounts or rewards in exchange for reviews. Keep it simple and genuine.</li>
  <li><strong>Respond to every review.</strong> Good or bad. A shop owner who replies to reviews looks professional and engaged. It also encourages future reviewers.</li>
</ul>

<p>Consistency matters more than volume. One new review a week is better than ten in one month and then nothing for a quarter. Automate the ask so it happens after every completed job, and the reviews will accumulate steadily.</p>

